  (no bundler) and [38;5;153mrequire()[39m in the Node test runner — so it ends with a small UMD-style footer: assign to
  [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m when [38;5;153mmodule[39m exists, otherwise hang a [38;5;153mPreferences[39m global off [38;5;153mself[39m. That footer is the only unusual thing
  in the file, and it exists because the repo has no build step, not as a preference.

  The module exports one factory plus its defaults:

  [33mcreatePreferences[39m(backend) -> { [33mget[39m(key), [33mset[39m(key, value), [33mgetAll[39m(), [33mclear[39m() }
  DEFAULTS  [32m// exported so tests assert against the real object, not a copy[39m

  [38;5;153mbackend[39m is anything with [38;5;153mgetItem[39m/[38;5;153msetItem[39m/[38;5;153mremoveItem[39m. The browser passes [38;5;153mwindow.localStorage[39m; tests pass a ~10-line
  [38;5;153mMap[39m-backed fake. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains one line: [38;5;153m<script src="prefs.js"></script>[39m [1mbefore[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, since [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reads the
  global at parse time.

  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m keeps its current shape — classic script, module-scope functions. It gains a single module-scope [38;5;153mconst prefs =
[39m  [38;5;153mcreatePreferences(window.localStorage)[39m.

  [1mData model[0m

  One key, [38;5;153mwebapp.prefs[39m, holding one JSON object:

  { [36m"v"[39m: [32m1[39m, [36m"rememberUsername"[39m: [34mfalse[39m, [36m"username"[39m: [31m""[39m }

  [38;5;153mDEFAULTS[39m is [38;5;153m{ rememberUsername: false, username: "" }[39m; [38;5;153mSCHEMA_VERSION[39m is [38;5;153m1[39m. Reads parse the stored string, then build
  the result as defaults overlaid with [1monly keys present in [38;5;153mDEFAULTS[0m — an unknown or stale key in storage is ignored
  rather than handed to the app, so a future rename can't resurrect old data. If the parse fails, the value isn't a
  plain object, or [38;5;153mv[39m isn't [38;5;153m1[39m, the read returns defaults and the bad value is left in place rather than deleted (a failed
  read shouldn't destroy data a later version might migrate).

  Writes serialize the whole document with the current [38;5;153mv[39m. [38;5;153musername[39m is stored only while [38;5;153mrememberUsername[39m is true;
  unchecking it clears the stored username in the same write, so there's no window where the box is off but the name is
  still on disk.

  Does this look right so far?

